{
  "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095879",
  "id": "q-124641",
  "status": "supported",
  "reason": null,
  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-124641",
    "knowledge_point_id": "1fd0b67f-681b-5b1d-8be9-2ab2d1ca5e66",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Image of a Polygon Under an Affine Transformation",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095879/assets/838861910879e95b0e1c3055da9f9597f194950c3823867a20a1e3c149354437.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v+1,\\,y=u-v+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$",
    "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v+1 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095879/assets/c89be48140d49ce8c9def44122aa2bfac779bdd02cfbfbe98f97d414a5637409.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(1,1),(3,2),(1,0),$ and $(-1,-1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095879/assets/d038caec0aa360e5d8cc1bcb4a3aa4d88408496d947b312d47634e51bbf4a11f.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving.",
